% foundations.p0 — i concetti FONDAMENTALI di informatica, matematica e scienze.
%
% Perche' un file a parte. «cosa e' una variabile» andava a muro con «Non so
% ancora molto su variabile», mentre `algebra_concept(variable, "a symbol
% representing an unknown value")` era gia' residente. Non era una lacuna di
% conoscenza: era il terzo asse di `question-emergence.md` §12 — la superficie
% italiana non raggiungeva l'entita' che il fatto ce l'aveva — e il muro ha
% dedotto «non so» da «non ho risposto», che e' proprio l'inferenza che §10.2
% dichiara illecita.
%
% Questi domini sono il caso in cui quell'errore si paga di piu': sono cio' che
% viene chiesto per primo, e in due lingue. Ogni concetto entra qui completo dei
% quattro pezzi che lo rendono raggiungibile e dicibile:
%
%   wiki_concept/3        la proposizione, in inglese
%   concept_gloss/3       la stessa proposizione nella lingua di chi chiede
%   semantic_topic_cue/2  le superfici che la raggiungono, in entrambe le lingue
%   tr/2 (in gloss.p0)    l'esonimo, quando canonicalizzare aiuta altri consumer
%
% Le definizioni sono conoscenza, non risposte a prompt: nominano il nucleo del
% concetto e non l'esempio con cui e' stato scoperto. Dove un termine ha due sensi
% (una variabile in programmazione e una in algebra) la riga li nomina entrambi
% invece di scegliere in silenzio: e' la stessa politica di `denotation.p0`.

% ── INFORMATICA ──
wiki_concept(variable, computing, "A variable is a named symbol standing for a value: in programming it labels a storage location whose content can change while the program runs, and in mathematics it stands for an unknown or varying quantity").
wiki_concept(loop, computing, "A loop is a control structure that repeats a block of instructions, either a fixed number of times or until a condition stops holding; without a change that eventually ends it, the repetition never terminates").
wiki_concept(recursion, computing, "Recursion is defining something in terms of itself: a recursive procedure calls itself on a smaller input and needs a base case that stops the descent, otherwise it never returns").
wiki_concept(compiler, computing, "A compiler translates a whole program from a source language into another language, usually machine code, before it runs; an interpreter instead executes the source directly, statement by statement").
wiki_concept(pointer, computing, "A pointer is a value that holds the address of another value in memory, so a program can reach or modify data without copying it; a pointer to no valid address is the classic source of crashes").
wiki_concept(array, computing, "An array stores a fixed number of elements of the same kind in consecutive positions, so any element can be reached directly by its index rather than by walking the others").
wiki_concept(stack_structure, computing, "A stack is a collection where the last item added is the first removed; programs use one to remember where to return from nested calls, which is why deep recursion can exhaust it").
wiki_concept(queue_structure, computing, "A queue is a collection where the first item added is the first removed, the order used whenever requests must be served in the sequence they arrived").
wiki_concept(data_structure, computing, "A data structure is a way of organizing data so that the operations a program needs most are cheap; choosing one trades the cost of lookup, insertion, deletion and memory against each other").
wiki_concept(sorting, computing, "Sorting arranges items in order by a key; comparison-based methods cannot beat n log n comparisons in general, so the practical choice depends on the data, the memory available and whether equal items must keep their original order").
wiki_concept(computational_complexity, computing, "Computational complexity describes how the time or memory a method needs grows with the size of its input, which is what decides whether it stays usable when the input gets large").
wiki_concept(source_code, computing, "Source code is the text of a program in a language people can read and edit; it is the artifact developers change, while the machine runs a translated or interpreted form of it").
wiki_concept(programming_language, computing, "A programming language is a formal notation for expressing computations, with a grammar a machine can parse and a semantics that fixes what each construct does").
wiki_concept(software_bug, computing, "A bug is a defect that makes a program behave differently from what it should; finding one means reproducing it, locating the cause and proving the fix by the same observation that showed the fault").
wiki_concept(debugging, computing, "Debugging is locating the cause of a defect by narrowing where the actual behaviour first diverges from the expected one, rather than by guessing at changes").
wiki_concept(compilation_error, computing, "A compile-time error is a fault the translator can see without running the program, such as a syntax or type violation; a run-time error appears only while it executes").
wiki_concept(bit, computing, "A bit is the smallest unit of information, one of two possible states usually written 0 and 1; eight bits make a byte, the usual unit for addressing memory").
wiki_concept(processor, computing, "A processor is the component that executes a program's instructions, fetching them in sequence and performing the arithmetic, comparisons and memory transfers they specify").
wiki_concept(random_access_memory, computing, "Working memory holds the code and data a program is using right now and loses its contents when power is lost, unlike storage, which keeps them").
wiki_concept(version_control, computing, "Version control records the successive states of a set of files, so any change can be attributed, compared with what came before and undone without losing the later work").
wiki_concept(object_oriented_programming, computing, "Object-oriented programming organizes a program around objects that keep their own data and expose the operations allowed on it, so the rest of the program depends on the interface rather than the representation").

wiki_concept(pump, engineering, "A pump moves a fluid by adding energy to it, either by trapping a volume and pushing it along or by accelerating it with a spinning impeller").
wiki_concept(lever, engineering, "A lever is a rigid bar turning about a fulcrum that trades distance for force, so a small effort over a long arm balances a large load over a short one").
wiki_concept(engine, engineering, "An engine converts stored energy into mechanical motion, usually by releasing it as heat or pressure and letting that drive a moving part").
wiki_concept(electric_current, physics, "An electric current is a flow of charge through a conductor, driven by a difference in potential and limited by the resistance of the path").

% ── MATEMATICA ──
wiki_concept(prime_number, mathematics, "A prime number is a whole number greater than 1 divisible only by 1 and itself; every whole number above 1 factors into primes in exactly one way, which is why they are the building blocks of arithmetic").
wiki_concept(equation, mathematics, "An equation states that two expressions are equal; solving it means finding the values of its unknowns that make the statement true").
wiki_concept(fraction, mathematics, "A fraction expresses a quantity as a number of equal parts of a whole, written as a numerator over a denominator").
wiki_concept(percentage, mathematics, "A percentage expresses a proportion out of one hundred, which makes shares of different totals directly comparable").
wiki_concept(derivative, mathematics, "The derivative of a function measures how fast its value changes as its input changes, and at a point it equals the slope of the tangent to the graph").
wiki_concept(integral, mathematics, "An integral accumulates a quantity over an interval and, for a curve above an axis, measures the area beneath it; integration and differentiation are inverse operations").
wiki_concept(matrix, mathematics, "A matrix is a rectangular array of numbers used to represent a linear transformation or a system of linear equations compactly, so composing transformations becomes multiplying matrices").
wiki_concept(vector_quantity, mathematics, "A vector is a quantity with both a magnitude and a direction, added tip to tail rather than by adding sizes alone").
wiki_concept(probability, mathematics, "Probability measures how likely an outcome is on a scale from 0 to 1, where the values of all mutually exclusive outcomes of one trial add to 1").
wiki_concept(arithmetic_mean, mathematics, "The mean is the sum of a set of values divided by how many there are; it is pulled by extreme values, which is why the median is often reported beside it").
wiki_concept(standard_deviation, mathematics, "The standard deviation measures how far values typically lie from their mean, so two sets with the same mean can be told apart by how spread out they are").
wiki_concept(factorial, mathematics, "The factorial of a whole number is the product of all whole numbers from 1 up to it, and counts the orderings of that many distinct items").
wiki_concept(infinity, mathematics, "Infinity is not a number reached by counting but a way of describing something without bound; sets can be infinite and still differ in size, as the whole numbers and the real numbers do").

% ── SCIENZE ──
wiki_concept(molecule, chemistry, "A molecule is two or more atoms held together by chemical bonds, and it is the smallest amount of a compound that still has that compound's properties").
wiki_concept(chemical_reaction, chemistry, "A chemical reaction rearranges atoms into new substances; the atoms are conserved, which is why a balanced equation has the same count of each element on both sides").
wiki_concept(force_concept, physics, "A force is an interaction that changes a body's motion; a net force produces an acceleration proportional to it and inversely proportional to the body's mass").
wiki_concept(energy_concept, physics, "Energy is the capacity to do work; it takes many forms and is converted between them, but in an isolated system the total is conserved").
wiki_concept(velocity, physics, "Velocity is how fast a body's position changes together with the direction of that change, which is why two bodies with the same speed can have different velocities").
wiki_concept(temperature, physics, "Temperature measures the average energy of the random motion of a substance's particles, and it decides which way heat flows when two bodies touch").
wiki_concept(cell_biology, biology, "A cell is the smallest unit that is alive on its own, enclosed by a membrane and able to take in energy, respond to its surroundings and reproduce").
wiki_concept(gene, biology, "A gene is a stretch of DNA that carries the instructions for a product, usually a protein, and the variants of a gene account for inherited differences between individuals").
wiki_concept(protein, biology, "A protein is a chain of amino acids folded into a shape that determines its job, from catalysing reactions to carrying substances and holding structures together").
wiki_concept(virus, biology, "A virus is a package of genetic material in a protein coat that cannot reproduce on its own and must enter a living cell and use its machinery to make copies").
wiki_concept(bacterium, biology, "A bacterium is a single-celled organism without a nucleus; most are harmless or useful, and only some cause disease").

